In `asn1_parse2`:
```c
j = ASN1_get_object(&p, &len, &tag, &xclass, length);
```
`ASN1_get_object` checks `*plength > (omax - (p - *pp))`. Here `omax = length` (the remaining length). So `len > length - header` sets the error bit. But even with the error bit set, `len` is still set to the parsed value (which could be huge). Let me re-read:

```c
if (*plength > (omax - (p - *pp))) {
    ASN1err(ASN1_F_ASN1_GET_OBJECT, ASN1_R_TOO_LONG);
    ret |= 0x80;
}
*pp = p;
return ret | inf;
```

So `*plength` (len) is set to the huge value, and the error bit is set. Then in `asn1_parse2`:
```c
j = ASN1_get_object(&p, &len, &tag, &xclass, length);
if (j & 0x80) {
    if (BIO_write(bp, "Error in encoding\n", 18) <= 0)
        goto end;
    ret = 0;
    goto end;
}
```
So if the error bit is set, `asn1_parse2` returns immediately. So `len` being huge doesn't matter here because it returns before using `len`.

Hmm. So in `asn1_parse2`, `len` is always bounded (error bit not set means `len <= length - header`).

